  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Above normal warmth into Saturday, then cooling \nclose to normal for next week. A pattern shift and several \nsystems may bring some relief to the drought by early next week,\nas shower and thunderstorm chances increase.\n\nAn upper level ridge of high pressure will work its way across\nthe area today and Friday, warming temperatures aloft allowing\nfor above seasonal temperatures. Looking at readings to climb\n10-15 degrees above normal with highs ranging from the 70s in\nthe mountains to the mid 80s in the piedmont. \n\nThe ridging breaks down over the weekend, with flow becoming\nmore zonal next week. This will support a busier weather pattern\nwith opportunity for frontal passages and potential for showers\nand thunderstorms. First opportunity is Saturday, followed by\nanother front Tuesday. It appears the atmosphere will be\ntrying to train itself to rain again...a quote that I heard from\none of the forecasters yesterday. The ground is so parched in\nplaces that it will take several rain events to prime the\nproverbial pump to sustain boundary layer moisture, an\ningredient needed for airmass showers and thunderstorms,\nand something that has been missing from our forecasts.\n\nThe showers that are being forecast are much needed, but let's\nnot get too carried away just yet. The atmosphere can be slow \nto change. Persistence and Newton's First Law of Inertia point \ntowards low QPF for the initial wave Saturday, which might look\npromising days in advance only to dry up at our doorstep. \n\nI would ask that we refrain from using extreme wording to\ndescribe general rainfall. A quote that I heard yesterday from \na local TV meteorologist referred to the showers that occurred \nyesterday as producing \"torrential\" downpours. Last I checked a \nfew hundredths of an inch is not a torrential downpour... so \nplease save that terminology until the atmosphere can support \nPWATS above 1.25 inches again, something I have not seen in a \nwhile.\n\nWith more clouds in the forecast for next week, along with the  \nrain at times, will mean temperatures should trend much closer \nto normal for this time of year. While we may have some \ninstability at times for thunderstorms, the probability for \nhazardous weather for our CWA appears low at this time.",
